    "case_id": "56",
    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old woman visited the nephrology outpatient clinic in November 2004 with symptoms of haematuria, proteinuria, and declining renal function. She reported intermittent macroscopic haematuria and colicky pain in the right upper quadrant of her abdomen. Her medical history included hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, and psoriasis. She had undergone an appendicectomy at age 16 and a cholecystectomy in 1984. She had a history of antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (IgM anticardiolipin antibodies) following a branch retinal artery thrombosis in September 2003 and a transient ischaemic attack in January 2004. Her medications included warfarin, atorvastatin, and perindopril, although the latter had recently been discontinued by her General Practitioner. During her initial renal clinic visit, her blood pressure was recorded at 164/90 mmHg. Urine dipstick testing showed blood (+++) and a 24-hour urine protein quantification of 0.52 g. Serum albumin was low at 31 g/l. An ultrasound scan showed normal kidneys with a small benign cyst on the left kidney. Both an IVU and cystoscopy were normal. Serum creatinine levels were 84 μmol/l in July 2003, 150 μmol/l in November 2004, and 300 μmol/l in January 2005. Further tests revealed a haemoglobin level of 8 g/dl. Serum folate levels were normal, but B12 and ferritin levels were low at 171 pg/ml and 33.7 ng/ml, respectively. Tests for gastric parietal cell antibodies and intrinsic factor antibodies were negative. ANA, ENA, ANCA, dsDNA antibodies, rheumatoid factor, and cryoglobulins were not detected. Serum angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) levels and complement (C3 & C4) were within normal ranges. Serology for Hepatitis B and C was negative. C-reactive protein levels were 4 mg/l. ESR and plasma viscosity were elevated at 76 mm/hr and 1.98 mPas, respectively. Serum electrophoresis showed no abnormalities, and Bence-Jones proteins were not found in a urine sample.\n\nElectron microscopy of a single glomerulus showed numerous fibrils with a diameter of 10.6–13.8 nm and within an expanded mesangial matrix. Light microscopy showed nine glomeruli, two of which were globally sclerosed. Of the remainder, many showed a mesangial matrix increase with hypercellularity, without the evidence of necrotizing lesions or fibrin thrombi. Silver stain showed basement membrane wrinkling, but no evidence of spikes or double contours. In a single glomerulus on one level, there was prominence of the epithelial cells of Bowman's capsule, possibly with some associated fibrin possibly representing an early crescent. Established crescents were not seen. There was a moderate and patchy interstitial inflammatory infiltrate. There was no interstitial fibrosis or evidence of tubular atrophy. Some tubules contained red cells. The arteries showed focal thickening of their walls. Immunofluorescence showed diffuse patchy granular mesangial and peripheral IgG (2+), C3 (+), and IgM (+). IgA and C1q were negative.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
